  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker is a free tool that shows your public IP address, the one every website you visit already receives, and explains what it means. It runs in your browser, needs no signup, and stores nothing on a server. It is an honest look at your IP, not a security scare.' },
  { q: 'What is an IP address?', a: 'An IP address is a numerical label assigned to every device on a network, used to identify it and route data to it, like a mailing address for internet traffic. There are two versions, IPv4 and IPv6. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows your current public address and which version it is.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between IPv4 and IPv6?', a: 'IPv4 addresses are 32 bits, written as four numbers like 192.0.2.1, allowing about 4.3 billion of them. IPv6 addresses are 128 bits, written in hexadecimal, allowing a vastly larger space. IPv6 exists because IPv4 ran out in 2011. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker displays whichever your connection uses.' },
  { q: 'Why does everyone in my house have the same IP?', a: 'Because your devices share one public IP through your router. Inside your network they use private addresses like 192.168.1.5, and the router uses NAT to represent them all with a single public address to the outside world. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows that shared public IP.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between a public and private IP?', a: 'A private IP is used inside your home or office network and is not routable on the public internet; RFC 1918 reserves ranges like 192.168.0.0/16 for this. A public IP is what the outside world sees. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows your public IP, since that is the one websites receive.' },
  { q: 'Can someone find my home address from my IP?', a: 'No, not from the IP alone. IP location is approximate, usually accurate only to a city or region. MaxMind, a geolocation provider, says its data is never precise enough to identify a household or street address. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker explains this so the number is not mistaken for a home address.' },
  { q: 'How accurate is IP-based location?', a: 'Roughly accurate to the country about 99.8% of the time, but only to the correct city around 66% of the time, per MaxMind. It reflects the routing area of your internet provider more than your exact spot. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker treats location as an estimate, not a precise fix.' },
