          <div class="seg b1">See it<small>what sites receive</small></div>
          <div class="seg b2">Understand it<small>public vs private</small></div>
          <div class="seg b3">In perspective<small>what it does not reveal</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">32 vs 128</div><div class="l">bits in an IPv4 versus an IPv6 address</div><div class="src">RFC 791, RFC 8200</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">Feb 2011</div><div class="l">when the last IPv4 addresses were handed out</div><div class="src">IANA / NRO</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">~66%</div><div class="l">how often IP location pins the right city</div><div class="src">MaxMind</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">Every site</div><div class="l">already sees your public IP, by design</div><div class="src">EFF</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <div class="t">On this page</div>
        <a href="#what">What it is</a>
        <a href="#what-ip">What an IP address is</a>
        <a href="#public">Public vs private</a>
        <a href="#reveals">What it reveals</a>
        <a href="#change">Changing your IP</a>
        <a href="#different">Why this tool</a>
        <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
      </nav>

      <article>
        <p class="lead"><strong>Your public IP address is not a secret you leaked, it is the return address every website necessarily receives, and on its own it reveals only your internet provider and an approximate location, not your name or exact street address.</strong> An IP checker simply shows you what sites already see. The Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/ip-address-checker/">IP Address Checker</a> displays your public IP and explains what it means, storing nothing on a server.</p>

        <p>This guide covers what an IP address actually is, the difference between IPv4 and IPv6, why your whole house shares one public address, what your IP does and does not give away, and how a VPN changes the picture. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and is a plain, honest look at a number people worry about more than they need to.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker is a free tool that instantly shows your public IP address, the one every website you visit already receives, and explains what it is. It works in your browser, needs no signup, and stores nothing on a server. Rather than dressing your IP up as a security scare, it shows you the number and puts it in honest perspective: what it reveals, what it does not, and how it fits into how the internet routes traffic.</p>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Shows what sites see.</b> Your public IP is the address servers use to send responses back to you, and this displays it.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Explains it plainly.</b> IPv4 or IPv6, public or private, and what each part means, without jargon.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Stores nothing.</b> There is no signup and no server-side log of your visit; it is a look, not a record.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">The basics</div>
        <h2 id="what-ip">What is an IP address, and what is IPv4 vs IPv6?</h2>
        <p>An IP address is a numerical label assigned to every device on a network, used to identify the device and route data to it, like a mailing address for internet traffic (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 791</a>). There are two versions in use. IPv4 addresses are 32 bits long, written as four numbers like <code>192.0.2.1</code>, which allows about 4.3 billion of them. IPv6 addresses are 128 bits, written as eight groups of hexadecimal like <code>2001:db8::8a2e:370:7334</code>, allowing a vastly larger space (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 8200</a>).</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>IPv4</th><th>IPv6</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Length</td><td>32 bits</td><td>128 bits</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Looks like</td><td><code>192.0.2.1</code></td><td><code>2001:db8::7334</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Address space</td><td>~4.3 billion</td><td>Vastly larger</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>IPv6 exists because IPv4 ran out. The global pool of unallocated IPv4 addresses was exhausted on 3 February 2011, when the last blocks were handed to the regional registries (<a href="https://www.nro.net/ipv4-free-pool-depleted/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Number Resource Organization, 2011</a>). IPv6's enormous space is the long-term fix. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows whichever version your connection is using.</p>

        <div class="cav">Two kinds of address</div>
        <h2 id="public">Public vs private: why does my whole house share one IP?</h2>
        <p>Because your devices use private addresses inside your network and share a single public one outside it. Certain ranges are reserved for private use and are never routed on the public internet: <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>, and <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>, the last of which is why your router hands out addresses like <code>192.168.1.5</code> (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 1918</a>). Your router then uses Network Address Translation (NAT) to represent every device behind it with one public IP, so a website sees a single address for your phone, laptop, and TV together (<a href="https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/learn/topics/networking/what-is-network-address-translation-nat.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cisco</a>).</p>
        <p>That public IP is assigned by your internet provider, and for most home connections it is dynamic, meaning it can change when you reboot your router or reconnect, rather than being a fixed label tied to you (<a href="https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cyberglossary/static-vs-dynamic-ip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fortinet</a>). This is worth knowing before assuming your IP is a permanent identifier.</p>

        <div class="cav">The real answer</div>
        <h2 id="reveals">What does your IP reveal, and what does it not?</h2>
        <p>Your IP reveals your internet provider and an approximate location, and little else on its own. Location from an IP is a broad estimate, not a pin on your house. MaxMind, a leading geolocation provider, reports that its data is accurate to the country about 99.8% of the time but only pins the correct city around 66% of the time within a 50-kilometer radius, and states plainly that IP data "is never precise enough to identify or locate a specific household, individual, or street address" (<a href="https://support.maxmind.com/knowledge-base/articles/maxmind-geolocation-accuracy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MaxMind</a>).</p>
        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">IP location is a rough guess, not a pin</figcaption>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 600 250" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: IP geolocation is about 99.8 percent accurate at the country level but only about 66 percent accurate at the city level.">
            <defs><linearGradient id="geograd" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1"><stop offset="0" stop-color="#C6603F"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#8a4429"/></linearGradient></defs>
            <line x1="70" y1="205" x2="530" y2="205" stroke="#E1D4be" stroke-width="2"/>
            <rect x="150" y="55" width="120" height="150" rx="8" fill="url(#geograd)"/>
            <text x="210" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="24" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">99.8%</text>
            <text x="210" y="228" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">Country level</text>
            <rect x="330" y="106" width="120" height="99" rx="8" fill="url(#geograd)" opacity="0.78"/>
            <text x="390" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="24" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">~66%</text>
            <text x="390" y="228" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">City level</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">Source: MaxMind (vendor accuracy figures, US city figure within a 50 km radius). An IP maps to a region, not a home address.</p>
        </figure>
        <p>What an IP does not reveal, on its own, is your name, your exact home address, your identity, or what you browse. As the Electronic Frontier Foundation puts it, an IP "doesn't necessarily reveal your identity," though it "can be combined with other pieces of information to identify you," and linking an address to a subscriber usually requires the provider records (<a href="https://ssd.eff.org/glossary/ip-address" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EFF</a>).</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Your IP can suggest</th><th>Your IP does not reveal, alone</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Your internet provider</td><td class="band">Your name</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An approximate city or region</td><td class="band">Your exact street address</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Whether you use a VPN or data center</td><td class="band">What you browse or type</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="cav">If you want to</div>
        <h2 id="change">Can you change or hide your IP?</h2>
        <p>Yes. Because most home IPs are dynamic, simply reconnecting can change yours over time. To deliberately mask it, a VPN routes your traffic through its own server, so websites see the VPN server's address instead of yours. Cloudflare describes it plainly: with a VPN, "the web servers will only log the IP address of the VPN server, which gives them no information about the user" (<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/access-management/what-is-a-vpn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare</a>).</p>
        <p>Whether that matters depends on your goals. If you are just curious what sites can see, the Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows it. If you want that visible address to be different, a VPN is the usual tool, though it moves your trust from your provider to the VPN provider rather than removing it.</p>

        <div class="cav">Why this one</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is Best Answer Hub different from other IP checkers?</h2>
        <p>The difference is honesty and clarity. Plenty of IP-lookup pages wrap your address in alarming language to sell a VPN or an antivirus. The Best Answer Hub IP Address Checker shows you the public IP that every site already receives, explains what it means in plain terms, and stores nothing about your visit. It treats your IP as a fact to understand, not a scare to monetize.</p>
        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">The honest framing</div><p>An IP checker cannot keep your address private, because your public IP is exactly what a server has to see to answer you. What this tool can do is be straight with you about that: show the number, explain it, and never turn it into a sales pitch or a stored record.</p></div>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">See what sites already see</span>
          <h3>Check your IP address</h3>
          <p>Instantly view your public IP, IPv4 or IPv6, with a plain explanation of what it means. No signup, nothing stored.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/tools/ip-address-checker/">Open the IP Address Checker</a>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about IP addresses</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <div class="qa">
              <div class="q" set:html={f.q}></div>
              <div class="a" set:html={f.a}></div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="cav">People also read</div>
        <h2>Keep going</h2>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/hash-generator/">Generate a Hash (SHA-256), Nothing Uploaded</a></b> Another network and security explainer, done in the browser.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/free-developer-tools/">Free Developer Tools That Run in Your Browser</a></b> The Developer Toolbox guide, all keeping your data local.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/free-online-tools/">70+ Free Online Tools, Nothing Uploaded</a></b> The overview of every Best Answer Hub hub.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/knowledge-center/smb-ai-readiness/">SMB AI Readiness Score</a></b> A free assessment with an instant radar and quick wins for your business.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>IETF, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 791, Internet Protocol</a>, 1981 (IPv4, 32-bit addresses).</li>
          <li>IETF, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 8200, IPv6 Specification</a>, 2017 (128-bit addresses).</li>
          <li>IETF, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 1918, Private Internets</a>, 1996 (private address ranges).</li>
          <li>Number Resource Organization, <a href="https://www.nro.net/ipv4-free-pool-depleted/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IPv4 free pool depleted</a>, 2011 (3 February 2011 exhaustion).</li>
          <li>Cisco, <a href="https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/learn/topics/networking/what-is-network-address-translation-nat.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What is NAT?</a> (one public IP shared by many devices).</li>
          <li>Fortinet, <a href="https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cyberglossary/static-vs-dynamic-ip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Static vs dynamic IP</a> (most home IPs are dynamic).</li>
          <li>MaxMind, <a href="https://support.maxmind.com/knowledge-base/articles/maxmind-geolocation-accuracy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Geolocation accuracy</a> (country ~99.8%, city ~66%, never a household).</li>
          <li>Electronic Frontier Foundation, <a href="https://ssd.eff.org/glossary/ip-address" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IP address</a> (an IP does not by itself reveal identity).</li>
          <li>Cloudflare, <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/access-management/what-is-a-vpn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What is a VPN?</a> (a VPN changes the IP sites see).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-bae-border-light text-sm text-bae-muted">More free tools: <a href="/tools/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">All Tools</a>, <a href="/tools/website-speed-test/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Website Speed Test</a>, <a href="/tools/hash-generator/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Hash Generator</a>, and the <a href="/developer-toolbox/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Developer Toolbox</a>.</p>
      </article>
